From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:50:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 609/609] arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini-station: allow UHS SDR50 on the
 microSD slot

At SD high speed the slot reads at about 23 MB/s, the limit of a 4-bit
bus at 48 MHz. Allow UHS SDR12, SDR25 and SDR50: let VMC, the I/O
supply, go to 1.8 V, and raise max-frequency to 100 MHz, which the
192 MHz source gives as 96 MHz. mtk-sd tunes SDR50 with the MT6797
data it already has.

The vendor driver also raises VMC by 40 mV through its trim field and
reprograms pad drive before the 1.8 V switch. Neither is done here.
Its 1.8 V input threshold, RDSEL 0, is what the firmware leaves.

SDR104 needs a faster clock parent and stays out.

Not yet run on hardware.
---
 .../mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts    | 21 +++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts
index 25d0abc..76e0475 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda-station.dts
@@ -562,9 +562,8 @@
 };
 
 /*
- * The vendor driver asks for VMCH as the card supply and VMC as the I/O
- * supply, both at 3.0 V. Hold both there: the first test runs the card at
- * 3.3 V signalling only, without the 1.8 V switch.
+ * The vendor driver asks for VMCH as the card supply at 3.0 V, and VMC as
+ * the I/O supply at 3.0 V, or 1.8 V for UHS signalling.
  */
 &mt6351regulator {
 	/*
@@ -581,7 +580,7 @@
 
 	mt6351_vmc_reg: ldo-vmc {
 		regulator-name = "vmc";
-		regulator-min-microvolt = <3000000>;
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <3000000>;
 	};
 };
@@ -609,21 +608,25 @@
 };
 
 /*
- * SD high speed at up to 50 MHz, 4 bits, 3.3 V signalling, no UHS. The
- * pads keep the firmware's drive and bias. The vendor reads the
- * card-detect pin high as a card present.
+ * Up to UHS SDR50, 4 bits: 1.8 V signalling and at most 100 MHz, which the
+ * 192 MHz source gives as 96 MHz. No SDR104 or DDR50. The pads keep the
+ * firmware's drive and bias; the vendor's 1.8 V input threshold is the
+ * firmware's setting already. The vendor reads the card-detect pin high
+ * as a card present.
  */
 &mmc1 {
 	pinctrl-names = "default", "state_uhs";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&mmc1_pins_default>;
 	pinctrl-1 = <&mmc1_pins_default>;
 	bus-width = <4>;
-	max-frequency = <50000000>;
+	max-frequency = <100000000>;
 	cap-sd-highspeed;
+	sd-uhs-sdr12;
+	sd-uhs-sdr25;
+	sd-uhs-sdr50;
 	cd-gpios = <&pio 67 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 	vmmc-supply = <&mt6351_vmch_reg>;
 	vqmmc-supply = <&mt6351_vmc_reg>;
-	no-1-8-v;
 	no-mmc;
 	no-sdio;
 	status = "okay";
